## classification.rules — Apache Jena generic-rule-syntax counterpart of
## gold/rules_reference.py. This is the authoritative, portable artifact
## (medallion_rdf_ido_strategy_mapping.md §6, §10 step 4): the artifact a
## real Jena/Fuseki deployment loads, one-to-one against the Python reference
## implementation this PoC actually runs and unit-tests. Diff the two on any
## change; both must be validated against the same fixtures
## (tests/test_rules_reference.py) before either is trusted.
##
## Scope (medallion §6 hybrid): LOCAL, node-local classification and
## directional guards only. The global connected-fragment partition
## (walk.py's fragment discovery, the C1/C2 consumer trace, fragment-merge)
## is NOT expressed here — SPARQL/Jena forward-chaining is awkward at
## "transitive closure that must stop at a node with a property"
## (medallion §6 point 5), and that partition stays in Silver/Python where
## it is already validated to ~97% source agreement.
##
## Reads ONLY graph:refdata and graph:masterdata. NEVER graph:oracle — see
## gold/oracle_guard.py, which is what makes that a structural invariant
## instead of a convention here.

@prefix pidsys: <https://pidsys.example/ns#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

# --- Fluid classification [data_specification.md §3.4] ---------------------

# A fluid with Category "Flare" is self-owning: forms its own flare
# collection system, never traced to a consumer.
[flareCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Flare")
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "flare")
]

# A fluid with Category "Utility" and a Subcategory containing "Steam" or
# "Condensate" is self-owning. Jena's builtin regex is the substring test
# data_specification.md §3.4 specifies (accepts a combined
# "Steam / Condensate" spelling too).
[steamCondensateCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Utility")
  (?fluid pidsys:subcategory ?sub)
  regex(?sub, 'Steam|Condensate')
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "steam_condensate")
]

[processCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Process")
  noValue(?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass ?any)
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "process")
]

# --- Flare guard [algorithm_spec.md §7.2] -----------------------------------
# If a fragment component's flow runs INTO a flare-fluid neighbour, and NOT
# the reverse, the neighbour is a discharge sink, not a consumer signal.
[flareGuardSkip:
  (?comp pidsys:isConnectedTo ?nb)
  (?comp pidsys:partOf ?seg) (?seg pidsys:fluidCode ?fluid)
  (?nb pidsys:partOf ?nbSeg) (?nbSeg pidsys:fluidCode ?nbFluid)
  notEqual(?fluid, ?nbFluid)
  (?nbFluidNode pidsys:fluidCode ?nbFluid) (?nbFluidNode pidsys:selfOwningClass "flare")
  (?comp pidsys:flowsTo ?nb)
  noValue(?nb pidsys:flowsTo ?comp)
  ->
  (?comp pidsys:skipAsConsumerSignal ?nb)
]

# --- Directional consumer guard [algorithm_spec.md §7.2a] -------------------
# Generalises the flare guard: if the NEIGHBOUR's flow runs into the
# fragment component (and not the reverse), the neighbour is a supply tying
# in — not a consumer this fragment feeds.
[directionalConsumerGuardSkip:
  (?comp pidsys:isConnectedTo ?nb)
  (?nb pidsys:flowsTo ?comp)
  noValue(?comp pidsys:flowsTo ?nb)
  ->
  (?comp pidsys:skipAsConsumerSignal ?nb)
]

# --- Relief-device attribution [algorithm_spec.md §7.4a] --------------------
# A relief device commissions with the side it protects (flow INTO the
# valve), not the discharge side (flow OUT of the valve).
[reliefAttribution:
  (?valve rdf:type ?cls) (?role pidsys:boundaryMember ?cls)
  regex(?role, 'relief')
  (?protectedSide pidsys:flowsTo ?valve)
  noValue(?valve pidsys:flowsTo ?protectedSide)
  ->
  (?valve pidsys:protectedBy ?protectedSide)
]
